<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Collective farming</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Communal farming, Collectivization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Collective farming is agricultural production in which multiple farmers run their holdings as a joint enterprise, as cooperatives of member-owners or as state farms owned and run by a central government; the process of aggregating farmland is called collectivisation. Older communal forms include the Aztec <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calpulli">calpulli</a> and the Russian peasant commune, the mir or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obshchina">obshchina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> introduced collective farms (kolkhozy) between 1927 and 1933, and after Stalin forced the peasants into collectives an estimated 5.7 to 8.7 million people died in the famine of 1932–1933. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> later collectivised, and the kibbutzim of Israel began in 1909.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Collective farming has older forms. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calpulli">calpulli</a>, local units that owned land and granted families the right to farm parts of it. The Wendat held land communally and let any person clear and farm it while they kept it in use. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obshchina">obshchina</a> or mir was a peasant community, as opposed to individual farmsteads. In Mexico a constitution of 1917 offered the development of ejidos, communal farms formed on land bought from large estates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> introduced collective farming in its republics between 1927 and 1933, and the Baltic states and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a>, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, adopted it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Leon Trotsky and the opposition had advocated voluntary cooperatives, and scholars distinguish his programme from the forced collectivisation Stalin carried out after 1928. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> collectivisation was attempted several times between 1948 and 1956, with police pressure, and succeeded only in the early 1960s under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janos-kadar">János Kádár</a>; by the early 1950s only a quarter of peasants had joined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>'s began in 1948 and was virtually complete in 1962, with force used at times. Polish collectivisation stopped in 1956.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, rural collectivisation began after the Party's 1953 &quot;general line for the transition to socialism&quot; and passed in six years through mutual aid groups, cooperatives and people's communes. Lin Chun notes that researchers agree it proceeded on a largely voluntary basis, without the violence of the Soviet case. In North Vietnam by 1960, 85 per cent of farmers and 70 per cent of farmland were collectivised. In Tanzania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>'s ujamaa began voluntarily and from 1973 to 1975 became forced villagisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>'s system collapsed in the late 1990s under drought, and estimates of deaths from starvation ran into the millions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Voluntary forms continue. Israel's kibbutzim began in 1909 as a combination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> and socialism, and in 2006 there were 267 kibbutzim and 40 moshavim shitufi'im, in which production is collective but consumption left to households. The Anabaptist Hutterites have farmed communally since the 16th century and now live mostly on the Canadian Prairies and the northern Great Plains. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> farmers generally keep their land privately and cooperate in processing and marketing; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> cooperatives hold about 70 per cent of the market.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Collective farming” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
